# Build Your First Agent

This tutorial builds an invoice Agent with a Flow route and an Action route, runs Turns on it as plain values, and then runs it live. An Agent holds state that changes across Signals.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "mikehostetler/new_jido", branch: "main"}
])
```

## Write the behavior

An Agent's behavior lives in Actions and Flows. Each one reads the current state from the `:agent_state` context key.

`ApplyPayment` is an Action that returns the complete next state. `PriceInvoice` is a Flow that returns the next state and the invoice total as two fields, and its `output_schema` declares both. An Action inside a Flow sees the same context.

```elixir
defmodule FirstAgent.ApplyPayment do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "first_agent_apply_payment",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{amount_cents: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.min(1)})

  @impl true
  def run(%{amount_cents: amount}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    paid = state.paid_cents + amount
    status = if paid >= state.total_cents, do: "paid", else: "open"
    {:ok, %{state | paid_cents: paid, status: status}}
  end
end

defmodule FirstAgent.SumLines do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "first_agent_sum_lines",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{lines: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.integer())})

  @impl true
  def run(%{lines: lines}, _context), do: {:ok, %{subtotal_cents: Enum.sum(lines)}}
end

defmodule FirstAgent.RecordTotal do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "first_agent_record_total",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{subtotal_cents: Jido.Joi.integer()})

  @impl true
  def run(%{subtotal_cents: subtotal}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    total = subtotal + div(subtotal * state.tax_percent, 100)
    {:ok, %{next_state: %{state | total_cents: total, status: "open"}, total_cents: total}}
  end
end

defmodule FirstAgent.PriceInvoice do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "first_agent_price_invoice",
    schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{lines: Jido.Joi.list(Jido.Joi.integer())}),
    output_schema:
      Jido.Joi.object(%{
        next_state: Jido.Joi.map(),
        total_cents: Jido.Joi.integer()
      })

  flow do
    step "sum", action: FirstAgent.SumLines, params: %{lines: input(:lines)}

    step "record",
      action: FirstAgent.RecordTotal,
      params: %{subtotal_cents: result("sum", :subtotal_cents)}

    output result("record")
  end
end
```

## Define the Agent

The `state` section declares the state contract. The `routes` section maps Signal types to behavior.

`as:` generates a Signal builder for a route. By default, a handler's complete output becomes the next state, as on the payment route. On the pricing route, `state from: output(:next_state)` selects one output field for the next state, and `reply` answers the caller with the total. These selectors read the declared `output_schema`, so a route that selects a field the schema does not declare fails at compile time.

```elixir
defmodule FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "first_agent_invoice",
    description: "Prices one invoice and tracks its payments"

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{
             tax_percent: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(10),
             total_cents: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0),
             paid_cents: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0),
             status: Jido.Joi.string() |> Jido.Joi.default("draft")
           })
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/billing/invoice"

    route "invoice.price", FirstAgent.PriceInvoice, as: :price do
      state from: output(:next_state)
      reply "invoice.priced", from: output(:total_cents)
    end

    route "invoice.payment.apply", FirstAgent.ApplyPayment, as: :apply_payment
  end
end

FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.definition().name
```

The cell returns `"first_agent_invoice"`.

## Create an instance

An instance adds an ID and complete state. The schema fills in the defaults, and no process starts.

```elixir
invoice = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.instantiate!(id: "inv-1001")

invoice.state
```

The cell returns `%{status: "draft", paid_cents: 0, total_cents: 0, tax_percent: 10}`.

## Run Turns as values

`cmd/3` runs one Turn. It returns the next Agent value and a list of Directives, and it performs none of them. Pass each result into the next Turn.

```elixir
price = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.price_signal!(%{lines: [1_200, 300]})
payment = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.apply_payment_signal!(%{amount_cents: 1_650})

{:ok, priced, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{}]} = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.cmd(invoice, price)
{:ok, settled, []} = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.cmd(priced, payment)

{priced.state.total_cents, settled.state.status}
```

The cell returns `{1650, "paid"}`.

An invalid Signal returns an error, and the Agent value you passed in still holds the same state.

```elixir
bad_payment = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.apply_payment_signal!(%{amount_cents: 0})

{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{}} = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.cmd(priced, bad_payment)

priced.state.paid_cents
```

The cell returns `0`.

A Signal type with no route also returns an error.

```elixir
unknown = Jido.Signal.new!("invoice.refund", %{}, source: "/billing/api")

{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{code: code}} = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.cmd(invoice, unknown)

code
```

The cell returns `:unmatched_input`.

## Run the Agent live

Start a Jido instance, then start the Agent inside it. The AgentServer holds the committed Agent and runs one Turn at a time.

```elixir
{:ok, _instance} = Jido.start(name: FirstAgent.Jido)
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(FirstAgent.Jido, FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent, id: "inv-2001")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(server)

{:ok, committed, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{}]} = Jido.AgentServer.call(server, price)

committed.state.total_cents
```

The cell returns `1650`, the same total as the pure Turn.

The server performs Directives after the commit. The Reply Directive sends the reply to the caller as a message.

```elixir
receive do
  %Jido.Signal{type: "invoice.priced", data: total} -> total
after
  1_000 -> :no_reply
end
```

The cell returns `1650`.

The state stays in the server, so each call builds on the last one.

```elixir
deposit = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.apply_payment_signal!(%{amount_cents: 650})
balance = FirstAgent.InvoiceAgent.apply_payment_signal!(%{amount_cents: 1_000})

{:ok, _agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(server, deposit)
{:ok, _agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(server, balance)

Map.take(Jido.AgentServer.agent(server).state, [:paid_cents, :status])
```

The cell returns `%{status: "paid", paid_cents: 1650}`.

`Jido.AgentServer.status/2` reports the Agent's identity and its `state_version`. Each committed Turn moved that version forward by one.

```elixir
status = Jido.AgentServer.status(server)

{status.agent_id, status.state_version}
```

The cell returns `{"inv-2001", 3}`.

A failed live Turn leaves the committed state and the server in place.

```elixir
{:error, %Jido.Error.ValidationError{}} = Jido.AgentServer.call(server, bad_payment)

{Jido.AgentServer.agent(server).state.paid_cents, Jido.AgentServer.status(server).state_version}
```

The cell returns `{1650, 3}`.

`Jido.stop_agent/3` stops one Agent. Its committed state goes with it, because this Agent has no persistence.

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(FirstAgent.Jido, "inv-2001")

Jido.whereis_agent(FirstAgent.Jido, "inv-2001")
```

The cell returns `nil`.

